lection for for persistence  mutation so then  definitely you would get other mutations  but if you would still combine the two  bit  but play around with the strength of  both let's say  i think it i think it might matter  because if your treatment is less severe  or  much more severe um  obviously it could be that some  mutations are specific for the severity  of of this treatment  all right thank you bro next question  why did you use aminoglycoside  antibiotics  yeah so so the amino glycosides um  they're they're one category of drugs um  that we used  we use that as a start and that's one of  the reasons why we also continue with it  but the better reason to to use a minor  glycosides is  that it's a bactericidal drug to start  with and that it is quite efficient to  kill stationary face cells and we really  wanted to work in stationary phase for  several reasons and i can go into that  later  uh for several several reasons but that  was one of the reasons that  we had to to well we our choices were  quite limited to be honest uh  fluoroquinolones would be another option  um we've not tried that yet in full um  full scale let's say um but the minor  glycosides were also the first where  where basically this evolution  was quite quite reproducible so so that  was one of the reasons as well  all right thank you bro next question  you have shown a graph that shows  disruptive selection in antibiotic  persistence  would this mean that even though the  microbiota that  microorganisms are of similar species  the drugs to kill these microbes will  differ  like a different drug or for another  extreme  so i believe um what what the  the the person who asked this question  means is that that i  have showed that evolution also works  for other species but  that indeed depending on the species but  also depending on the drug that we use  the absolute starting level is a bit  different maybe also the absolute end  level might be a bit different  the reason why we chose different amino  glycosides is just  to make make our conclusions more more  generally  valid let's say but there we do see  different even different end levels  so for example gentamicin we we  see that in the lab as a very very  potent aminoglycoside  resulting in very low levels um  very low levels at the start but also  lower levels at the end  and for the pathogens um yeah there we  chose the antibiotic that at the start  resulted in  in at least a biphasic killing pattern  um for example for  pseudomonas we used i think gentamicin  absolute monotherogenosa started at a  very very low level  with this type of of treatment if you  would use other drugs and other  concentrations this starting level might  differ a bit and maybe the end level as  well but i think by now  because of all the other groups that  also use evolution experiments  the conclusions would largely remain the  same for those kind of experiments  it looks like we have one last question  here brahm  why did you use stationary phase for the  evolution experiments  yeah so so we at a given point i had to  choose anyway but we used  a stationary phase in design of this  evolution experiment  but as i showed you many others used  other setups  other made other choices  the reason that we used stationary phase  is both practical but also  biological practical  because stationary faith if you if you  from one day to another verify a bit  you're still in stationary phase and  although i do realize that  early stationary phase or or or 20 hours  later  that can still matter a lot but if you  are still  changing one or two hours in your setup  it's probably not the biggest  deal while if you do that in exponential  phase or upon dilution in fresh medium  like like the other group  groups do that can can change a lot  another practical reasons that we chose  stationary phase is that if we treat an  exponential phase well we kill  almost all of our cells with these kind  of treatments  and i was already worried in stationary  phase that we would be on the  on the on the edge of what would be  viable because after a while  i do not want to start with 100  populations in flasks  and then after a while only have one or  two viable populations  left so so actually that was one of the  reasons to work in stationary phase  because the levels are generally higher  there  anyway but also because because um it  was also one of the reasons why i worked  in those large volumes then  later ethel proved that that was  actually not so important  sadly uh so i i did a lot of work for  nothing  but then the biology behind it  we i also tend to believe that if these  are very artificial i realize right but  if we continue to study bacteria in mid  exponential  phase then probably the relevance to in  vivo conditions where  mid exponential faith i don't i don't  believe that there is such a thing as  mid exponential phase  i'm not saying that they're all in  stationary phase in vivo for sure not  but to me this sounds a bit  or resembles a bit better what might be  happening in vivo where  conditions are far from optimal to grow  exponentially  so that that's a biological reason to to  use the stationary phase in fact all  right well  thank you it looks like we don't have  any more questions um thank you bro  do you have any final comments for our  audience today  um no no no just that we that we are  very happy with the uh with the scripta  platform and and that's obviously why we  acquired  the onyx uh commercial machine now as  well and  i'm very anxious to see it in real life  uh  i didn't yet because of corona measures  but i will in this in  the near future so i'm looking forward  to it  well thank you again braun van  vandenberg for your time today  and for your important research we would  also like to thank  lab roots and our sponsor in scriptdeaf  for underwriting today's educational  webcast  before we go i'd like to thank the  audience for joining us today and for  their inte